00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:02.105
it was, was in, was in a great decline as
the manufacturing industry went away.

00:05:02.105 --> 00:05:04.460
Shirt-making industry went to the south.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:07.750
The munitions industry at the end of the
Vietnam War basically shut down.

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:09.450
And there was nothing left.

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:10.790
Really nothing left literally.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:12.760
It was a awful.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:17.120
So so we had to we, we, we needed to.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:20.450
We, you know, we knew we, economic
development was gonna be a major theme.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:21.960
But we couldn't just jump into that.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:23.820
We were just, we were not trusted.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.670
So the issue was winning the bona fide's
of community leaders.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.160
Of the political apparatus and

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:34.260
of the other community organizations in
the neighborhoods and in the clergy who

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.790
have important political influence
particularly in the black community.

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:43.370
So we, we, we set about it by with
volunteerism and really mobilizing.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:45.730
The, what was already happening,

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:49.140
but organizing it in much more systematic
ways.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:53.760
So basically, I made, I had every dean of
every school rec, you know,

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:57.460
told that it's part of your job
description to have urban outreach and

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.580
to get your students and faculty involved.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:03.300
So the drama school is doing improv
theaters in near by neighborhood and

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.470
the forestry environmental studies schools
planting trees and

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:10.750
creating pocket parks in neighborhoods,
and, and we could go on and on.

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:14.960
All kinds of, of initiatives on parts of
of students and faculty.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:19.650
And, and after a couple years that, it
really helped, we, we were really kind of,

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:21.100
beginning to win the hearts and minds.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:24.840
Hey, Yale's different now, it's really
engaged in helping the city and

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.190
then being a participating citizen.

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:32.450
And then it was only at that point that
the mayor came to me and said, you know,

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:36.190
we need to fix the downtown and the only,
the only entity in the place with cap,

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:42.070
with the capital and the vision and the
self-interest to make that work is Yale.

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:46.310
So, with my blessing, go ahead and buy
downtown, and redevelop it.

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:49.320
And I mean, that was amazing, just from a
historical point of view,

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:50.840
that we had that opportunity.

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:52.290
But it worked out really well.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:53.520
And we've got great retail.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.900
Great, great food, you know,

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:58.680
really terrific restaurants in the
downtown New Haven now.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:01.320
It's actually, truthfully, I hate to say
this, but

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.895
it's actually nicer than Harvard Square.

00:07:02.895 --> 00:07:06.826
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] We won't compare.

00:07:06.826 --> 00:07:09.815
But you know, I think one thing that you
were saying Rick,

00:07:09.815 --> 00:07:14.152
that you know, this, this idea of how you
work with the faculty at your school.

00:07:14.152 --> 00:07:15.406
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:07:15.406 --> 00:07:17.730
&gt;&gt; I think is really interesting.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:19.540
Faculty are, are, generally tenured.

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:20.720
You can't fire them.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.550
You can't force them to do what you want.

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:26.370
So, how did you go about creating a vision
and

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:30.430
getting them to, to really have a
consensus on that?

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:32.550
&gt;&gt; Well this, it, all, all, this is all
true for

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:36.070
almost any constituency where you need
their support.

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:38.850
I mean, this would be true to any of you
who are leading organizations and

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:42.450
you wanna keep the, you know, your senior
staff with you,

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:43.890
you wanna keep the employees with you.

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:47.060
Basically, a leader needs to articulate a
vision.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:51.260
He needs to be clearly and coherently
articulated.

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:54.100
It needs to be tested with key people at

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.880
the beginning before it's widely
communicated.

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.200
But then it's communicate, communicate,
communicate.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:03.490
I mean it's just getting the message out
and, and going and

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:06.820
patiently talking about it with skeptical
audiences.

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:07.880
Answering questions.

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:12.270
Being, being open and responsive to
suggestions and change.

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:14.830
But also not yielding on the core vision.

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:15.510
And, you know, one,

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:18.580
one of the great things about faculty is
they're basically rational.

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:21.360
And, and even though they're very
independent.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:24.475
And so, persuading is possible, actually.

00:08:24.475 --> 00:08:25.100
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; If you have,

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:29.590
if you, you know, if it's a compelling
argument eventually, you'll win.

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:34.140
And, and, that, I think that's, that's,
that was the way, you know, for

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:39.150
things like, you know, investing in the
infrastructure, you know, investing in

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:44.460
the city and internationalization, all of
those strategies.

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:48.085
Really became I think a pretty common
consensus with time,

00:08:48.085 --> 00:08:52.738
patience, explanation, lots of meetings
with groups to talk it through.

00:08:52.738 --> 00:08:56.460
&gt;&gt; Hm, the on, on the topic of
internationalization.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.690
You did face some push back on the
decision by the University to

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:05.100
open a campus in Singapore in partnership
with the National University of Singapore.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:10.750
There was a lot of question around whether
Yale should be partnering with a country

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:15.770
that had some questionable stands on human
rights in,

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:17.825
in conflict with the way Yale felt.

00:09:17.825 --> 00:09:21.775
[CROSSTALK] And so how did you make that
decision to really put

00:09:21.775 --> 00:09:27.309
yourself behind that decision, when you
knew there was going to be public outcry?

00:09:27.309 --> 00:09:31.189
&gt;&gt; Right, well the si, so the initiative
to start a liberal arts college in

00:09:31.189 --> 00:09:34.640
Singapore came very, you know, rather late
in my presidency.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:40.750
We started talking about it in spring of
2009 after I'd been in my position for

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:42.230
nearly 16 years.

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:45.080
And and it was, it was a kind of

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:49.380
a capstone of a major effort to do a lot
of other things, globally.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:51.020
Lots of exchange programs.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:55.550
Research facilities in China, all kinds of
other things have preceded this.

00:09:55.550 --> 00:10:00.190
So, the actual internationalization battle
for hearts and

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:02.480
minds within the university had already
been won.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.730
What, what hit the nerve of some of our
faculty, and

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:08.390
I, I hate, leaders will always say this.

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:11.400
But it was a really small minority of
people who were

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:12.790
really exorcised about this.

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:16.890
The majority, as one, as one of my key
advisors,

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:21.390
one faculty member I've greatly respect
said to me about this before it blew up.

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:22.430
He said.

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:26.500
He said you know the thing is you've got
15% of the people wildly enthusiastic

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:31.370
about this and 5% are, are just really
upset about it.

00:10:31.370 --> 00:10:35.960
And the remaining 80% actually don't know
or care or just totally indifferent.

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:40.330
He said, what you have to avoid is a
situation where the 5% somehow get

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:43.010
an issue where they can bring, where they
can persuade the 80%.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:46.100
Well that was so pressured because it
turned out to be what happened.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:51.460
The, the, the fa, the, these faculty
members tried to pass a resolution

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:56.080
that said we, you know, we support full
human rights in Singapore.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:57.650
Well, who's gonna vote against that?

00:10:57.650 --> 00:10:59.010
Right?
So it wa, it wa,

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:02.520
it was, it, it, it was not that they
censured our doing.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:04.610
And it was not a vote of no confidence in
our doing it.

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:09.780
It was very clever, and, and you know, so
we took a few lumps in the press on this.

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:13.160
Let me tell you why this is such a great
idea though.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:14.740
Asia is crying out for

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:19.276
the kind of critic pedagogy that focuses
on critical thinking.

00:11:19.276 --> 00:11:25.040
You know, China trains e, enorm, enormous
numbers of engineers and

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:28.900
we, and Americans worry that they're going
to eat our lunch some day because of this.

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:30.710
And they look at us and they say, but

00:11:30.710 --> 00:11:34.550
all the innovation in the world is coming
from the United States.

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:35.600
And why?

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:38.020
They have a very different method of
educating people.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:39.000
They teach people to think for

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.110
themselves, to be independent, to question
the teacher.

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:43.660
To, to speak up in class.

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:44.280
To argue.

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:46.660
We need more of that.

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:49.390
And so the Chinese were kinda begging me
to do this.

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:51.260
To set up a liberal arts college within
China.

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:56.000
I thought that was an environment where it
just wasn't ready for, for this kind of,

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.360
of really free, free ranging intellectual
battles and questioning authority.

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:05.150
But Singapore frankly for all the
reputation of it of the,

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:10.620
they're trying to maintain a kinda tenuous
hold on a, on a, on what is a peten,

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:15.980
you know, what is essentially an island,
multicultural island in the middle of,

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:20.450
you know of a, a very dif, a very
different, you know,

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:25.260
ethnic pu, composition of Malaysia and
China nearby.

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:29.010
You know, yes the government has been
authoritarian in many ways and

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:31.850
the freedoms that we that we would like to
that we enjoy are not

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:33.590
present entirely there.

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:36.840
But the universities that have always been
in Singapore are kind of

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:39.090
oasis where the rules are different.

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:40.420
And where free speech goes on.

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:44.710
Where academic freedom is practiced and
friends of ours said,

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:48.230
look this, if you are negotiating an
arrangement with the Singaporeans

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:53.950
you can trust them to abide by the,
they'll do, they'll be straight.

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:56.780
If you write it down in contract, they'll
honor it.

00:12:56.780 --> 00:13:00.490
And so we wrote, we wrote a great contract
which essentially protects it

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:04.070
academic freedom on the campus.

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:07.140
It doesn't protect students who wanna go
march on the public in the public

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:10.680
square downtown because that's not allowed
in the city.

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:11.560
We have to have a permit.

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:17.318
But, but we can we have them define our
campus as an indoor space.

00:13:17.318 --> 00:13:20.559
So it was one was one [UNKNOWN] And just,

00:13:20.559 --> 00:13:26.053
just a few weeks ago there was an there
was a good example of this, a film,

00:13:26.053 --> 00:13:31.638
with, with this politically, critical of
the, of the ruling party,

00:13:31.638 --> 00:13:38.073
was released in Europe, and it was banned
in, for public exhibition in Singapore.

00:13:38.073 --> 00:13:41.770
But before the public ban occurred, we had
already assured,

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:44.200
been assured by the government.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:46.230
