00:27:17.570 --> 00:27:18.740
&gt;&gt; Of course.

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:20.135
&gt;&gt; But there were some made earlier.

00:27:20.135 --> 00:27:21.820
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Why even ask?

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:29.100
&gt;&gt; No, I, I I I think, I think there that
we,

00:27:29.100 --> 00:27:35.430
we have we have tried to, to work with our
partners, the Universities.

00:27:35.430 --> 00:27:41.160
To, to, to renegotiate certain aspects of
our arrangements with them and

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:47.110
we've run into situations where they've
been very adamant about maintaining one or

00:27:47.110 --> 00:27:51.860
two of the terms in our original agreement
which I fully understand why it recog,

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:55.010
why it recognizes their interests but on
the other hand.

00:27:55.010 --> 00:27:58.480
You know, it's not, not consistent with
sort of, internet norms.

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:05.770
We, we're an internet company, and there
are, there are how, what happens.

00:28:05.770 --> 00:28:06.950
Here's a good example.

00:28:06.950 --> 00:28:08.800
What happens, this is one we just had a
hard time.

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:14.540
We, we would like to do more to
essentially be able to take down.

00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:19.770
Comments on this, on our, in our
interactions on our forums.

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:24.920
When, if, they start to get harassing or
threatening or, or, or are, we've

00:28:24.920 --> 00:28:30.480
had some, there are some bad actors, we've
had incidents that are bad incidents.

00:28:30.480 --> 00:28:34.020
You know, and, the University perspective
is that we don't take anything down,

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:38.650
we just, we just, you know, we, we, we, we
and certainly it's my policy at Yale,

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:44.000
if, you know, hate speech is responded to
with more speech.

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:47.050
You're basically, you, you tea, use it as
a teaching moment.

00:28:47.050 --> 00:28:50.300
You tell the community, this is
inappropriate.

00:28:50.300 --> 00:28:51.740
But we're, we're not gonna, we're not
gonna take it,

00:28:51.740 --> 00:28:53.420
you know we're not gonna take it down.

00:28:53.420 --> 00:28:54.740
But we're, but we're gonna tell you,

00:28:54.740 --> 00:28:56.500
we think this is inappropriate and here's
why.

00:28:58.356 --> 00:29:00.770
The internet's not like that.

00:29:00.770 --> 00:29:05.130
All the big social media places you know
are taking stuff down all the time.

00:29:05.130 --> 00:29:07.430
And, and, you know, so getting,

00:29:07.430 --> 00:29:09.470
the Universities really aren't ready to
accept that.

00:29:09.470 --> 00:29:11.980
They want, they want the University kind
of rules to govern them.

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:16.580
They don’t want an, of Coursera, taking
anything down without their involvement.

00:29:16.580 --> 00:29:20.350
And so we made em, we made kind of a
mistake reaching for this, I think.

00:29:20.350 --> 00:29:22.140
But, that we backed off and.

00:29:22.140 --> 00:29:25.630
Okay, we won't do anything without talking
to you first, but it's just the way,

00:29:25.630 --> 00:29:27.930
it's kind of give and take, you see, in
these kinds of situations.

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:32.870
&gt;&gt; You've got a room full of potential
entrepreneurs here,

00:29:32.870 --> 00:29:36.660
as well as, people who have a real passion
for education.

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:40.844
So, outside of what Coursera is doing,
what do you see,

00:29:40.844 --> 00:29:45.751
as the biggest opportunity for disruption
in education today, and what

00:29:45.751 --> 00:29:51.671
advice would you have for the students
here on how to pursue those opportunities.

00:29:51.671 --> 00:29:53.462
&gt;&gt; Well doing, yes this,

00:29:53.462 --> 00:29:59.115
this new technology does have the
potential to disrupt higher education.

00:29:59.115 --> 00:30:00.647
But I think actually-

00:30:00.647 --> 00:30:03.134
The near term phenomenon, the phenomena
for

00:30:03.134 --> 00:30:07.063
the next decade won't be that universities
as we know them disappear,

00:30:07.063 --> 00:30:11.510
it'll be that universities actually have
their impact amplified.

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:14.590
And, and, and become more important to
society by

00:30:14.590 --> 00:30:18.950
virtue of having even more people you
know, that they're engaged with.

00:30:18.950 --> 00:30:20.700
So that's one thing.

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:22.840
But what, but what in, what in ed tech?

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:25.700
I mean, you know, the promise that ed tech
is

00:30:25.700 --> 00:30:29.470
going to deliver miracles has been around
for a long time.

00:30:29.470 --> 00:30:34.660
I remember when I started teaching as an
assistant professor at Yale,

00:30:34.660 --> 00:30:39.070
all the excitement about what Carnegie
Mellon then called programmed learning.

00:30:39.070 --> 00:30:42.601
That, you know, we're going to replace
lectures entirely with

00:30:42.601 --> 00:30:45.617
computer drills that are actually all
print based, but

00:30:45.617 --> 00:30:49.363
due to this basically take you through
material in a programmed way.

00:30:49.363 --> 00:30:52.794
And if you get a wrong answer here, it
leads you to this branch, and

00:30:52.794 --> 00:30:54.696
you get the following material, and

00:30:54.696 --> 00:30:58.930
if you get a right answer it goes to this
path, and, it, that never happened.

00:30:58.930 --> 00:30:59.890
It's still a good idea.

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:06.020
I mean, a modern version of this would be
an idea I had a Yale actually that we

00:31:06.020 --> 00:31:10.410
couldn't get high school teachers to
accept which was we wanted to do a,

00:31:10.410 --> 00:31:14.250
have Yale Press do a really first-rate
Chinese language,

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:16.750
you know, teaching Chinese to English
language learners.

00:31:16.750 --> 00:31:17.670
And my idea was gamify it.

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:23.410
You know, it was basically have, have
voice recognition technology, and

00:31:23.410 --> 00:31:26.640
have, and have you say something, you've
confronted an obstacle, in,

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:29.010
in a game you know ,and, and

00:31:29.010 --> 00:31:32.890
you have to communicate the words orally
and the computer would fix it.

00:31:32.890 --> 00:31:37.910
I thought that would be a great way to
teach, you know, to teach language.

00:31:37.910 --> 00:31:42.640
The high school teachers we consulted with
didn't even want to touch it, so.

00:31:42.640 --> 00:31:46.300
It's gonna be a long time before there's
really revolutionary disruption in

00:31:46.300 --> 00:31:47.350
traditional education.

00:31:47.350 --> 00:31:51.110
In the meantime, all this stuff added
around the edges is gonna, like,

00:31:51.110 --> 00:31:53.962
like moves for university level stuff.

00:31:53.962 --> 00:31:59.662
And like the Khan Academy in material and
other things that focus more on K to 12.

00:31:59.662 --> 00:32:01.820
I think it's going to be greatly enriching
for people.

00:32:01.820 --> 00:32:04.020
It's gonna make their lives better.

00:32:04.020 --> 00:32:07.060
I think it'll, I think it's creating great
social value.

00:32:07.060 --> 00:32:09.420
I mean I'd encourage you to think about
you know,

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:12.980
joining a startup and try to help build
something.

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:16.480
The idea that we're gonna completely
disrupt the educational system,

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:20.050
as opposed to just reaching more people
who are outside it, or

00:32:20.050 --> 00:32:23.800
improving the experience of those who are
inside it is probably just a little early.

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:28.690
Well I've been speaking a lot here and
asking you a lot of questions, Rick, so

00:32:28.690 --> 00:32:31.690
I want to make sure to open it up to our
audience and

00:32:31.690 --> 00:32:38.260
we have two mics that will be passed as
well as our hashtag gsbvftt.

00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:42.230
So we'll be alternating between questions
from the audience here and

00:32:42.230 --> 00:32:46.120
questions that Ryan will read from
Twitter.

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:47.926
So, Brian, do you have one ready to go?

00:32:47.926 --> 00:32:51.549
&gt;&gt; Sure, yeah.

00:32:51.549 --> 00:32:56.853
So we've gotten a few questions from the
audience on on Coursera and

00:32:56.853 --> 00:32:58.330
on your role at Yale.

00:32:58.330 --> 00:32:59.170
&gt;&gt; Mm.
&gt;&gt; And one that keeps up

00:32:59.170 --> 00:33:01.800
that you might have an interesting
perspective on is,

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:03.906
what's your pitch to colleges or
universities?

00:33:03.906 --> 00:33:08.630
Is it solely about amplifying the message
of universities?

00:33:08.630 --> 00:33:11.645
How do you deal with the threats they,
they perceive Coursera or

00:33:11.645 --> 00:33:16.650
MOOCs having to the traditional model and
is there a, a place for a sort of a moral

00:33:17.930 --> 00:33:21.930
responsibility of universities to provide
education to the masses?

00:33:21.930 --> 00:33:26.820
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so that is, I mean, the, the, the
scale that this offers has been my,

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:29.380
my principle pitch to universities.

00:33:29.380 --> 00:33:32.690
And it's actually a har, it's actually a
kind of a hard sell.

00:33:32.690 --> 00:33:33.952
I mean it's not, it's, it's.

00:33:33.952 --> 00:33:38.830
[COUGH] I, I think part of what I, what I
have to do personally is, is to,

00:33:38.830 --> 00:33:40.110
is to carry that message.

00:33:40.110 --> 00:33:43.120
I mean I, I think that it's the, and, and

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:46.330
one of the advantages of having been a
university president and

00:33:46.330 --> 00:33:50.200
dealing now with university leaders is I
do have some credibility.

00:33:50.200 --> 00:33:54.460
So people are kinda surprised when I say
my first, you know, the first,

00:33:54.460 --> 00:34:00.495
the first thing about this is not
improving on campus teaching although it,

00:34:00.495 --> 00:34:02.729
it's great for that and will help you do
that.

00:34:04.160 --> 00:34:05.560
If that's what you focus on,

00:34:05.560 --> 00:34:07.790
you're sort of missing what's really, what
really matters.

00:34:07.790 --> 00:34:11.530
So I have been pushing primarily the
notion that

00:34:11.530 --> 00:34:14.410
this should redefine the mission of
university.

00:34:14.410 --> 00:34:16.770
Our mission, the mission of the university
in,

00:34:16.770 --> 00:34:19.540
if you go to most people's missions
statements, will have something to

00:34:19.540 --> 00:34:23.600
do with the creation and the dissemination
of knowledge.

00:34:23.600 --> 00:34:26.270
Okay, so we create knowledge through our
research.

00:34:26.270 --> 00:34:29.030
We disseminate it through our publications
and teaching.

00:34:29.030 --> 00:34:32.040
But we publish to increasingly small
audiences.

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:35.260
You know, I mean, many professionals
publish their articles and

00:34:35.260 --> 00:34:37.730
are read by, you know, 100 people in their
field.

00:34:37.730 --> 00:34:40.550
And that's pretty good.

00:34:40.550 --> 00:34:42.500
We disseminate it through teaching.

00:34:42.500 --> 00:34:43.240
Okay.

00:34:43.240 --> 00:34:46.740
And the teaching, in many places, like
GSB, is wonderful.

00:34:46.740 --> 00:34:47.970
But how big are the classes?

00:34:47.970 --> 00:34:50.930
And how many students do, does a professor
reach?

00:34:50.930 --> 00:34:54.850
This offers a op, you know, you know on
average maybe 100 or

00:34:54.850 --> 00:34:59.820
200 students a year is the, is the contact
a faculty member has.

00:34:59.820 --> 00:35:02.910
And with the Coursera of course you reach
tens of thousands.

00:35:02.910 --> 00:35:08.350
And, and that's revolutionary I think, and
it gives the university

00:35:08.350 --> 00:35:12.960
a chance to make a difference to wor, you
know global development.

00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:16.770
To, to give economic development
opportunity to people all over the world.

00:35:16.770 --> 00:35:20.310
And to give life long learning opportunity
to those who you know,

00:35:20.310 --> 00:35:23.200
don't need the economic advancement, but
just are curious.

00:35:23.200 --> 00:35:25.030
Just want to learn more, want to grow.

00:35:25.030 --> 00:35:27.660
So it's an amazing, I think it's amazing.

00:35:27.660 --> 00:35:28.770
That is basically my pitch.

00:35:28.770 --> 00:35:31.990
I mean, yes, we'll support your on-campus
with classrooms.

00:35:31.990 --> 00:35:33.130
I mean, it's great.

00:35:33.130 --> 00:35:34.770
It's, yes we'll give you data.

00:35:34.770 --> 00:35:35.870
You can analyze it.

00:35:35.870 --> 00:35:40.100
You can figure out which questions in your
quizzes are, are not working.

00:35:40.100 --> 00:35:42.680
And you can improve your lectures that
lead up to those questions.

00:35:42.680 --> 00:35:45.700
There are a whole lot of things we can do
that way.

00:35:45.700 --> 00:35:50.550
And yes we can generate revenue for you,
but frankly, we think we can at least, but

00:35:50.550 --> 00:35:54.190
frankly that revenues not, the scale at
which we can do that is not gonna be

00:35:54.190 --> 00:35:58.130
a game changer for 128 different
university departments.

00:35:58.130 --> 00:36:02.320
We had even if we were distributing half a
billion dollars,

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:07.520
that's not a huge number for universities
you know to have so.

00:36:07.520 --> 00:36:08.560
I think it's mainly the,

00:36:08.560 --> 00:36:12.940
it's mainly think about about what, what
is the social role of the university?

00:36:12.940 --> 00:36:17.700
And technology has enabled universities to
move beyond the education of

00:36:17.700 --> 00:36:22.910
the elites of highly selected elites, to
the, the, the world.

00:36:22.910 --> 00:36:25.070
And I think that's incredibly powerful.

00:36:27.260 --> 00:36:28.500
A question from the audience.

00:36:31.240 --> 00:36:34.830
&gt;&gt; I, I'd love to ask about challenging
decisions you had to face kind of at

00:36:34.830 --> 00:36:36.220
the end of your time at Yale.

00:36:36.220 --> 00:36:38.690
you, you sorry I'm, I'll stand up so you
can.

00:36:38.690 --> 00:36:39.520
&gt;&gt; Oh yeah thanks.
&gt;&gt; You were one of

00:36:39.520 --> 00:36:42.220
the hardest things you had to deal with at
the end of

00:36:42.220 --> 00:36:45.340
your tenure at Yale was expanding the
residential college system.

00:36:45.340 --> 00:36:48.540
And obviously running into the financial
crisis.

00:36:48.540 --> 00:36:52.010
The the economics of that became
challenging.

00:36:52.010 --> 00:36:52.620
&gt;&gt; Yep.
[LAUGH].

00:36:52.620 --> 00:36:54.810
&gt;&gt; It was really rumored that one of the
other view for

00:36:54.810 --> 00:36:58.690
the top speaker's offered a very large
gift in order to, you know, name one of

00:36:58.690 --> 00:37:04.020
those colleges after that person, and it
was rumored that Yale turned that down.

00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:06.300
I realize that that conversation is
probably confidential and

00:37:06.300 --> 00:37:08.830
don't want you to have to delve too much
into that.

00:37:08.830 --> 00:37:12.370
But can you talk a little bit about the
challenge you have as an executive

00:37:12.370 --> 00:37:15.180
between profitability and kind of
organizational values and

00:37:15.180 --> 00:37:19.180
how you think that situation potentially
exemplifies that and how you

00:37:19.180 --> 00:37:22.880
think about that at Coursera as you
obviously deal with a number of, kind of,

00:37:22.880 --> 00:37:26.900
important morals and values in education
but also needing to drive profitability?

00:37:28.080 --> 00:37:29.140
&gt;&gt; Good question.

00:37:31.220 --> 00:37:32.745
So you know, we, we, we have, have.

00:37:33.980 --> 00:37:36.750
We put a lot of money into our facilities
and it's only after we

00:37:36.750 --> 00:37:41.230
get half mostly renovated that we decided
expansion would be a good idea.

00:37:42.640 --> 00:37:46.560
You know, if you know the Yale system we
have these very, particularly since

00:37:46.560 --> 00:37:50.590
they've been renovated, amazing facilities
to house undergraduates that are.

00:37:52.150 --> 00:37:54.120
That create social environments and

00:37:54.120 --> 00:37:58.790
great communities and have amazing you
know, facilities to support that.

00:37:58.790 --> 00:38:01.130
We wanted to build two more.

00:38:01.130 --> 00:38:05.620
It was high, high on the agenda right
before the crisis hit.

00:38:05.620 --> 00:38:09.090
And along with a lot of other capital
projects that we

00:38:09.090 --> 00:38:12.250
just basically froze at that point.

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:17.980
I, I won't confirm or deny the, the rumor
you heard.

00:38:17.980 --> 00:38:21.590
But I will say this, I think that, so we
basically committed,

00:38:21.590 --> 00:38:25.700
decided at the time of the financial
crisis that if we were going to go

00:38:25.700 --> 00:38:28.354
forward with colleges, we're going to have
to raise 100% of the money.

00:38:28.354 --> 00:38:32.350
We, we, we, that we going to have to be
essentially an all cash transaction.

00:38:32.350 --> 00:38:35.540
We weren't gonna take new debt to finance
the new colleges.

00:38:35.540 --> 00:38:39.270
And we would have to absorb the
incremental operating cost and

00:38:39.270 --> 00:38:39.870
operating budget.

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:42.830
We would have to have enough, you know,
projected future surplus so

00:38:42.830 --> 00:38:43.410
we could do that.

00:38:43.410 --> 00:38:47.970
So all, it was, it we were not gonna rob
anything existing in order to,

00:38:47.970 --> 00:38:49.270
in order to expand.

00:38:51.480 --> 00:38:56.660
There's no question that had we wanted to
allow

00:38:57.720 --> 00:39:01.970
donors to name these colleges, we probably
could have raised a substantially greater,

00:39:01.970 --> 00:39:07.390
we, we, we're, you know, we're naming
towers and, and courtyards and entryways.

00:39:07.390 --> 00:39:10.240
But the, the names of the Yale colleges,
you know,

00:39:10.240 --> 00:39:14.100
originally were selected by the Yale
Corporation to honor, you know,

00:39:14.100 --> 00:39:19.660
important founders of the university or
great Yale, you know, Yale-worthies.

00:39:19.660 --> 00:39:21.260
Great, great, great citizens and

00:39:21.260 --> 00:39:25.730
contributors to public life or
intellectual life who came from Yale.

00:39:25.730 --> 00:39:30.230
And my board did not wanna name a college
for a donor.

00:39:30.230 --> 00:39:33.770
And I, I, I have to say.

00:39:33.770 --> 00:39:37.350
I, I ha, I was very ambivalent actually on
that subject personally,

00:39:37.350 --> 00:39:40.510
because I really wanted to see this
project go forward and

00:39:40.510 --> 00:39:44.180
we have lots of things named for donors
at, at, at, at Yale, but

00:39:44.180 --> 00:39:48.970
I, I saw the argument and I, and it was a
pretty overwhelmingly I mean the,

00:39:48.970 --> 00:39:54.030
the Yale Corporation which is a small,
very highly deliberative body.

00:39:54.030 --> 00:39:55.260
Was pretty strong about this.

00:39:55.260 --> 00:40:00.190
And so we had to disappoint at least one
prospective donor, who,

00:40:00.190 --> 00:40:01.610
who would have, who would have done this.

00:40:03.760 --> 00:40:04.950
I maybe, I guess, didn't answer it.

00:40:04.950 --> 00:40:10.340
The general question is yes, there's
always a tension between values and prac-,

00:40:10.340 --> 00:40:11.350
and practicality.

00:40:11.350 --> 00:40:16.400
But I, I have to say, at an institution
like Yale, the choice comes down on

00:40:16.400 --> 00:40:20.630
the side of values way more often than
not.

00:40:20.630 --> 00:40:23.780
And, and and actually I would say this

00:40:25.240 --> 00:40:29.340
is probably more salient for all of you,
take it out of the Yale context.

00:40:29.340 --> 00:40:33.259
I mean, one of the things I always ask
myself.

00:40:34.450 --> 00:40:35.840
It whenever we had a tough issue,

00:40:37.250 --> 00:40:40.300
any, any tough issue was what is the right
thing to do.

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:44.560
I mean that is you can make all kinds of
expedient arguments on one

00:40:44.560 --> 00:40:45.730
side or another.

00:40:45.730 --> 00:40:50.010
And it's the temptations when you're
running a big institution like Yale.

00:40:50.010 --> 00:40:52.720
Because it is so much in the public view
and so

00:40:52.720 --> 00:40:57.240
much of what you do is scrutinized by so
many different people there is always

00:40:57.240 --> 00:41:02.370
a temptation to say, oh, this is one we
can sort of hide.

00:41:02.370 --> 00:41:03.470
We don't have to deal with it.

00:41:03.470 --> 00:41:05.550
We can shove it under the rug.

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:07.070
You can't, you just can't.

00:41:07.070 --> 00:41:08.100
There is nothing,

00:41:08.100 --> 00:41:12.380
nothing gets shoved under the rug, I mean
basically, you have to assume.

00:41:13.400 --> 00:41:17.860
I, I, I live my life like I had to assume
that everything I did or

